  "narrative": {
    "intro": "This report covers federal contract obligations in Texas for FY2026 to date. Texas has received $93.5B in contract obligations through this fiscal year to date, spread across 184,730 awards with an average award size of $506K. Obligations are up 8.2% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $42.7B, followed by the Department of Homeland Security with $26.7B and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $17.9B. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry by obligations at $18.2B, followed by Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $13.5B and Aircraft Manufacturing at $12.6B. LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION and MCKESSON CORPORATION are the top contractors by obligations, receiving $14.1B and $12.2B respectively, with an additional $9.8B listed for LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION. Fiscal-year comparisons show rising totals from FY21 through FY26 with $55.1B in FY21 and $93.5B in FY26. This brief summarizes the buyers, vendors, industries, and year-over-year movement for federal spending in Texas.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations in Texas rose year over year by up 8.2%. Total obligations for FY2026 to date reached $93.5B, after $86.4B in FY25 and $59.3B in FY24. Award volume shows 184,730 awards with an average award size of $506K, reflecting both scale and higher aggregate spending."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries dominate federal obligations in Texas?",
        "body": "Commercial and Institutional Building Construction leads industries with $18.2B in obligations. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing and Aircraft Manufacturing follow with $13.5B and $12.6B respectively. Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing accounts for $12.2B and Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing accounts for $4.9B."
      }
    ]
  },
